<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day24" xml:id="day-24-lecture">
  <title>Day 24</title>

  <introduction>

    <p>
        Ch 9.1: Maximum Margin Classifier (MMC)
    </p>
  </introduction>

<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>What is a hyperplane?
      <ul>
        <li><p>How do you mathematically describe a hyperplane?</p></li>
        <li><p>How do you mathematically describe the two sides of a hyperplane?</p></li>
        <li><p>Given the equation of a hyperplane and the coordinates of a point, determine which side of the hyperplane the point is on.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>What qualifies a hyperplane as a separating hyperplane?
      <ul>
        <li><p>Describe this mathematically using an inequality.</p></li>
        <li><p>Determine whether a hyperplane is a separating hyperplane given a graph, or given the equation of the hyperplane and the coordinates and classes of a few points.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>How do you use a separating hyperplane as a classifier?</p></li>
    <li>
      <p>What makes a hyperplane a maximal margin hyperplane?
      <ul>
        <li><p>What are its margin and support vectors?</p></li>
        <li><p>Given a graph, clearly label the margin and support vectors. Also infer the size of the margin from the graph.</p></li>
        <li><p>Describe the optimization problem mathematically.</p></li>
        <li><p>Given the equation of the maximal margin hyperplane and the coordinates of support vectors, calculate the size of the margin by hand.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Recognize MMC as a hard margin classifier and describe its sensitivity to new points.
            </p>
          </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
  <title>The goal</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 50%">
    <image source="figures/SVM-example">
      <shortdescription>An example of a support vector machine for classification.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Today is all about classification.</p></li>
          <li><p>The goal is to find a line in the middle that separates the two classes. There are lots of options, but we want the one that stays as far away from everyone as possible.</p></li>
          <li><p>For today, we need to use <m>y\in\{-1,1\}</m> for this to make sense.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>What is a hyperplane?</title>

  <image source="figures/hyperplanes_p1_p2_p3" width="90%">
    <shortdescription>Three figures showing hyperplane examples for Euclidean dimension 1 (hyperplane is shown as a point), dimension 2 (hyperplane is shown as a line), and dimension 3 (hyperplane is shown as a 2D plane).</shortdescription>    
  </image>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>In <m>p</m>-dimensional space, a hyperplane is a flat affine subspace of dimension <m>p-1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Affine means it does not have to pass through the origin.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Mathematical definition of a hyperplane</title>
  <p>
    <md>
      \beta_0+\beta_1X_1+\beta_2X_2+\cdots+\beta_pX_p=0
    </md>
  </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Being on the hyperplane means having a point <m>X=(X_1,X_2,\cdots,X_p)^T</m> satisfying this equation.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The vector <m>\beta=(\beta_1,\beta_2,\cdots,\beta_p)^T</m> is the normal vector to the hyperplane.
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      If <m>\boldsymbol{\beta}=(\beta_1,\beta_2,\cdots,\beta_p)^T</m> is normalized such that <m>\sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j}^2 = 1</m>, then <m>\boldsymbol{\beta}</m> represents the unit normal vector to the hyperplane.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      The <term>signed perpendicular distance</term> from a point <m>\mathbf{x}_i=(x_{i1},x_{i2},\cdots,x_{ip})^T</m> to the hyperplane is given by 
                      <md>
                        \frac{\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}}{\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j}^2}}=\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}
                      </md>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>
          <li component="slides-inst-notes"><p>Show that when <m>p=2</m>, this is just a line.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
<aside>
  <title>Dot product</title>
  
  <p>
    The quantity
    <md>\beta_{0} + \beta_{1} x_{1} + \beta_{2} x_{2} + \ldots + \beta_{N} x_{N}</md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Is the same as
    <md>
      \begin{bmatrix} \beta_0 \amp \beta_1 \amp \beta_2 \amp \ldots \amp \beta_N \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1\\x_1\\x_2\\\vdots\\x_N\end{bmatrix}
    </md>
    
  </p>
<p>
  Is the same as
  <md>
    \begin{bmatrix} \beta_0 \\ \beta_1 \\\beta_2 \\ \vdots \\ \beta_N \end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix}1\\x_1\\x_2\\\vdots\\x_N\end{bmatrix}
  </md>
  
</p>



  <p>
    Is the same as
    <md>\beta_{0} + \boldsymbol{\beta}^{T} \mathbf{x}</md>
  </p>

  <p>
          where
          <m>\mathbf{\beta}=\begin{bmatrix}\beta_{0} \\ \beta_{1} \\\beta_{2} \\ \vdots \\ \beta_{N}\end{bmatrix}</m>
          and
          <m>\mathbf{x}= \begin{bmatrix}1\\x_{1}\\x_{2}\\\vdots\\x_{N}\end{bmatrix}</m>
  </p>
  
</aside>
</paragraphs>

<example>
  <title>Signed perpendicular distance</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For the shown data points and hyperplane, sketch the <m>f(x^*)</m> for each of the shown points and indicate whether its positive (+ve) or negative (-ve). How confident are you in your classification of each point? Why?
    </p>
    <image source= "figures/sketch_fxstar_ex1" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Data points from two classes with a line separating them. Three data points are labeled as a, b, and c.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      The figure shows the values returned by each point when substituted into the hyperplane equation. The sign of the value indicates which side of the hyperplane the point is on, and the magnitude indicates how far away it is from the hyperplane. 
    </p>
    <p>
      We are more confident in the classification of points that are further away from the hyperplane, and less confident in the classification of points that are closer to the hyperplane. Therefore, wer are most confidnent in the classification of point c, and least confident in the classification of point a.
    </p>
        <image source= "figures/sketch_fxstar_ex1_sol" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Data points from two classes with a line separating them. Three data points are labeled as a, b, and c. Sketched lines indicate the the value returned by f(x*) for each point along with its sign.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </answer>
  
</example>

<example>
  <title>Hyperplane with <m>p=2</m></title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The equation for the shown hpyerplane is 
      <md>1+2X_1+3X_2=0.</md> 
    </p>
    <p>
      For each of the given point, determine whether the point is above, on, or below the boundary. Justify your answers.
    </p>
      <image source="figures/Fig9-1.png" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>A hyperplane separating two regions with blue and purple colors.</shortdescription>
      </image>


  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>(X_1,X_2)=(-0.5, 0).</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Substitute into the equation of the hyperplane to obtain <m>1+2(-0.5)+3(0)=0</m>. Therefore, the point is on the boundary.
      </p>
    </answer>    
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>(X_1,X_2)=(0, -1).</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Substitute into the equation of the hyperplane to obtain <m>1+2(0)+3(-1)=1-3=-2</m>. Since this is less than 0, the point is below the boundary.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>(X_1,X_2)=(0, 1).</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Substitute into the equation of the hyperplane to obtain <m>1+2(0)+3(1)=1+3=4</m>. Since this is greater than 0, the point is above the boundary.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
</example>



<paragraphs>
  <title>There are two sides to every hyperplane</title>

    <p>
      <md>
        \beta_0+\beta_1X_1+\beta_2X_2+\cdots+\beta_pX_p&lt;0
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
        \beta_0+\beta_1X_1+\beta_2X_2+\cdots+\beta_pX_p&gt;0
      </md>
    </p>

  <image source="figures/Fig9-1.png" width="50%">
    <shortdescription>A hyperplane separating two regions with blue and purple colors.</shortdescription>
  </image>

  <p>
    We can use the separating hyperplane to design a classifier.
  </p>
</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Classification Setup</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>Data matrix:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          X=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          - &amp; x_1^T &amp; -\\
          - &amp; x_2^T &amp; -\\
          &amp; \vdots &amp; \\
          - &amp; x_n^T &amp; -
          \end{pmatrix}_{n\times p}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          x_1=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          x_{11}\\
          \vdots\\
          x_{1p}
          \end{pmatrix},
          \cdots,
          x_n=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          x_{n1}\\
          \vdots\\
          x_{np}
          \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>Observations in one of two classes, <m>y_i\in\{-1,1\}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          Y=
          \begin{pmatrix}
          y_1\\
          y_2\\
          \vdots\\
          y_n
          \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
  <p>Separate out a test observation:</p>
  <p>
    <md>x^*=(x_1^*\;\cdots\;x_p^*)^T</md>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Separating Hyperplane</title>
  <p>
    <md>
      \begin{aligned}
      \beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}&gt;0
      &amp;\text{ if }y_i=1\\
      \beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}&lt;0
      &amp;\text{ if }y_i=-1
      \end{aligned}
    </md>
  </p>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 55%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-2a.png">
      <shortdescription>Three separating hyperplanes separating two classes of observations.</shortdescription>
    </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Linearly separable data.</p></li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Two-class classification. 
                </p>
            </li>
          <li><p>No points can be on the wrong side of the line (no noise, and no outliers).</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Another way to say it</title>
  <p>
    <md>
      \begin{aligned}
      \beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}&gt;0
      &amp;\text{ if }y_i=1\\
      \beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}&lt;0
      &amp;\text{ if }y_i=-1
      \end{aligned}
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>For all <m>i</m>:</p>
  <p>
    <md>
      y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip})&gt;0
    </md>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Separating hyperplane becomes a classifier</title>

    <image source="figures/Fig9-2b.png" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A separating hyperplane used as a classifier.</shortdescription>
    </image>
      <p>If you have a separating hyperplane:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Check</p>
            <p>
              <md>
                f(x^*)=\beta_0+\beta_1x_1^*+\beta_2x_2^*+\cdots+\beta_px_p^*
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>If positive, assign <m>\hat{y}=1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>If negative, assign <m>\hat{y}=-1</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>


</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>How do we pick?</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-2a.png">
      <shortdescription>Three separating hyperplanes separating two classes of observations.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>There's lots of separating hyperplanes here, so how do we find a "best" one?</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the perpendicular distance from each training observation to a given separating hyperplane.</p></li>
          <li><p>The smallest such distance is the <m>margin</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Distance from an observation to a hyperplane</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/SVM-example-WithLine1">
      <shortdescription>SVM example with line 1.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/SVM-example-WithLine2">
      <shortdescription>SVM example with line 2.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Maximal margin classifier</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-3.png">
      <shortdescription>Maximal margin classifier.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The <term>maximal margin hyperplane</term> is the hyperplane with the largest minimum distance to the training observations.</p></li>
          <li><p>Classifying using it gives you the <term>maximal margin classifier</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Observations that are closest are called <term>support vectors</term>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Support vectors satisfy <md>Y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1X_{i1}+\beta_2X_{i2})=M.</md></p></li>
          <li><p>If points moved slightly, then the maximal margin hyperplane would move.</p></li>
          <li><p>There is no effect if any other points moved.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<example>
  <title>MMC demonstration example</title>
  <introduction>    
    <image source="figures/SVM-DesmosExample.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>MMC demonstration example.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement><p>Sketch the maximal margin hyperplane.</p></statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement><p>What is the equation of this line in the form <m>\beta_0+\beta_1X_1+\beta_2X_2=0</m>?</p></statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>X_{1} + X_{2}- 4 = 0</md>
                </p>
            </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement><p>Circle the support vectors. What is their distance from the line?</p></statement>
      </task>
      <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Classify the points <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(4,4)</m>, and <m>(2,2)</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
            Points are at distance <m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>. Build the isoceles right triangle with <m>d</m> on two sides and 1 for hypotenuse.
        </p>
      </answer>
      </task>
<figure>
  <interactive desmos="tklbommiwz" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
    <shortdescription>Interactive graph for the maximal margin classifier exercise.</shortdescription>
  </interactive>
  <caption>Explore the separating hyperplane and identify the support vectors.</caption>
</figure>
</example>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Mathematical Formulation</title>

    <p><md>
  <mrow>\amp \underset{\beta_0, \beta_1, \dots, \beta_p, M}{\text{maximize}} M</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \text{subject to } \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2 = 1,</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp y_i(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{i1} + \beta_2 x_{i2} + \dots + \beta_p x_{ip}) \ge M \quad \forall i = 1, \dots, n</mrow>
</md>
</p>


      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              MMC requires perfect linear separation, so it is a <term>hard margin classifier</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p><m>M</m> ensures every point is at least distance <m>M</m> from the hyperplane.</p></li>
          <li><p>Because of the multiplication by <m>y_i</m>, this also requires that the point is on the correct side of the hyperplane.</p></li>
          <li><p>The last equation forces points to be on the correct side of the hyperplane if <m>M&gt;0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Making <m>M</m> as big as possible gives the maximal margin hyperplane.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>First constraint</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 65%">
    <image source="figures/SVM-DesmosExample-WithLine.png">
      <shortdescription>SVM demonstration example with a separating line.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>We had the equation <m>-4+X_1+X_2=0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>We can divide by any number and get the same line.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\sum_{i=1}^p\beta_i^2=1^2+1^2=2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>We get the same sloped line if we divide by <m>\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^p\beta_i^2}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>We just need to solve for the intercept.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>-\frac{4}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}X_1+\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}X_2=0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>-2\sqrt{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}X_1+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}X_2=0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Check that <m>\sum_{i=1}^p\beta_i^2</m> is now 1.</p></li>
          <li><p>Plug in the support vector <m>x_i=(1,4)</m>, which gives <m>y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1X_{i1}+\beta_2X_{i2})=1/\sqrt{2}=:M</m> (the margin).</p></li>
          <li><p>Note that <m>y=1</m> corresponds to the red Xs, and <m>y=-1</m> corresponds to the blue dots below the line.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Second constraint</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 65%">
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/SVM-DesmosExample-WithLine.png">
        <shortdescription>SVM demonstration example with a separating line.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Blue circles: <m>y_i=-1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Red Xs: <m>y_i=1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>-2\sqrt{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}X_1+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}X_2=0</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <md>y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2})\geq M</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>For point <m>(3,2)</m>:
            <ul>
              <li><p><m>y_i=1</m>.</p></li>
              <li><p><m>1\left(-2\sqrt{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 3+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 2\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.</p></li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For point <m>(0,3)</m>:
            <ul>
              <li><p><m>y_i=-1</m>.</p></li>
              <li><p><m>-1\left(-2\sqrt{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 0+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 3\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.</p></li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For point <m>(1,1)</m>:
            <ul>
              <li><p><m>y_i=-1</m>.</p></li>
              <li><p><m>-1\left(-2\sqrt{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 1+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot 1\right)=\sqrt{2}</m>.</p></li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

