<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Consider the following dataset with two predictors,
      <m>X_1</m> and <m>X_2</m>, and a binary response variable
      <m>Y</m>.
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Observation</cell>
        <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>Y</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>1</cell><cell>1.4</cell><cell>8.2</cell><cell>+1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>2</cell><cell>2.0</cell><cell>6.0</cell><cell>+1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>3</cell><cell>7.1</cell><cell>4.0</cell><cell>+1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>4</cell><cell>6.0</cell><cell>3.0</cell><cell>+1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>5</cell><cell>1.3</cell><cell>2.0</cell><cell>-1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>6</cell><cell>2.0</cell><cell>3.5</cell><cell>-1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>7</cell><cell>4.2</cell><cell>0.5</cell><cell>-1</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>8</cell><cell>6.0</cell><cell>0.5</cell><cell>-1</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      Suppose observations 2, 4, 6, and 8 are the support vectors.
      The maximal margin classifier is given by
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>0.6X_1+0.8X_2-5=0.</m>
    </p>
    <!-- <p>
      Recall that the coefficients satisfy
      <m>\beta_1^2+\beta_2^2=1</m>, and the support vectors
      satisfy
    <md>
      Y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1X_{i1}+\beta_2X_{i2})=1.
    </md>
    </p> -->
  </introduction>
  <task>
  <statement>    
    <p>
      Using the given information, calculate the margin <m>M</m>, as defined in our book and class notes: the minimum perpendicular distance from the observations to the separating hyperplane.
    </p>
    </statement>
  <solution>
    <image source="figures/MMC_checkpoint" width="70%">
      <shortdescription>Data points from two classes plotted on the X1, X2 predictor space. The separating hyperplane and the support vectors are identified, and the (equal) distance from each support vector to the hyperplane is shown.</shortdescription>
      
    </image>
  <p>To determine the margin <m>M</m>, substitute a support vector into the margin-boundary equation. Recall that support vectors satisfy
    <md>Y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1X_{i1}+\beta_2X_{i2})=M.</md>
  </p>
  <p>Using observation 2, which is a positive support vector with coordinates <m>(X_1,X_2)=(2,6)</m> and <m>Y=1</m>, we obtain</p>
  <p>
    <md>1(0.6(2)+0.8(6)-5)=M,</md>
    <md>1.2+4.8-5=M,</md>
    <md>M=1.</md>
  </p>
  <p>Therefore, the two margin boundaries are</p>
  <p>
    <md>0.6X_1+0.8X_2-5=1</md>
    <md>0.6X_1+0.8X_2-5=-1.</md>
  </p>
  <p>The margin is <m>M=1</m>, which is the perpendicular distance from the separating hyperplane to either margin boundary. The full distance between the two boundaries is <m>2M=2</m>.</p>
</solution>

  </task>

</exercise>

<paragraphs>
  <title>But what if....</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-4.png">
      <shortdescription>Example of a dataset for which no separating hyperplane exists.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>...there is no separating hyperplane.</p></li>
          <li><p>This means that there is no solution for <m>M&gt;0</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Sensitivity to new points</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-5a.png">
      <shortdescription>First example illustrating sensitivity of the maximal margin classifier to new observations.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig9-5b.png">
      <shortdescription>Second example illustrating sensitivity of the maximal margin classifier to new observations.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Even if there is a separating hyperplane, it might not be a good one.</p></li>
      <li><p>Add a blue observation on the right.</p></li>
      <li><p>This causes a dramatic shift in the maximal margin hyperplane.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    This calls for a more flexible approach, which we will discuss next time.
  </p>
</paragraphs>

</handout>